          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01W4LgcdPvPpchzP8TFPjFd3",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/consistent-hashing.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Consistent Hashing\\ndescription: A distributed hashing scheme that minimizes key remapping when cluster membership changes.\\ntimestamp: 2026-08-17T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - pattern/Distributed-Systems\\n  - concept/Hashing\\n  - concept/Caching\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single source; consistent hashing is a well-established pattern in distributed systems, but no corroborating sources provided.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nConsistent hashing is a distributed hashing technique used in distributed caches, load balancers, and peer-to-peer systems. It maps both cache keys and cache nodes onto the same circular keyspace, allowing efficient cluster membership changes.\\n\\n## Key Property: Minimal Key Remapping\\n\\nThe defining characteristic of consistent hashing is that adding or removing a node only remaps the keys that fall between the changed node and its neighbour. This contrasts with naive modular hashing, which would reshuffle the entire keyspace when cluster size changes\\u2014a catastrophic problem for cache coherency and efficiency.\\n\\n## Virtual Nodes\\n\\nA small number of physical nodes on the ring creates uneven load distribution. **Virtual nodes** address this by assigning several ring positions to each physical node, smoothing out the load imbalance and improving cache utilization across the cluster.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Distributed systems caching and cluster membership concepts (source: eval://log-no-day-grouping/sample-04)\\n\u0022}"
        }
